#### Trigger

The purpose of the trigger is to detect Au-Au interactions, detect p-p interactions, locate a Au-Au collision vertex within \(\pm 6\) cm in less than 200 ns and detect multiple events occurring within 40 ns. Figure 2.25 is a quadrant

Figure 2.24: A schematic diagram of the configuration of the external TPC’s [ST 92].

view of the STAR detector showing the trigger detectors. The trigger will be able to select high multiplicity events, select events whose particle distributions suggest inhomogeneity, select events having unusual concentrations of strangeness and provide a calibration event trigger, a cosmic ray event trigger and a high multiplicity trigger.

The trigger system is divided into two components. The first is the trigger detectors, consisting of a central trigger barrel (CTB), vertex position detectors (VPD), TPC endcap MWPC's, veto calorimeters (VIC) and output from the EMC. The second component consists of the data processing and triggering functions and the interfaces to the detector subsystems and Data Acquisition.

Figure 2.25: Quadrant view of the STAR detector system showing baseline trigger detectors: EM Calorimeter (EMC), Central Trigger Barrel (CTB), Vertex Position Detector (VPD), TPC endcap MWPC’s and Veto Calorimeter (VC) [ST’92].

The CTB is a barrel of 200 slats of scintillator on the TPC outer surface. These slats are 1.5 cm thick x 6.25 cm wide x 4.2 cm long. The slats are placed in the center to one another to form a cylinder around the TPC. Each slat is optically decoupled to two proximity-focusing mesh dynode phototubes.

The VPD contains two scintillators each placed on either end of the TPC. The light signals are read out by photomultiplier tubes. The resulting signal is sent to specially designed fast circuitry to determine the vertex location. This circuit uses the time difference in signals to determine the location of the primary vertex in z to within \(\pm\)6 cm in less than 200 ns.

The EMC measures a fraction of the total transverse energy which is streams mostly from photons and electrons which result from \(\pi^{0}\) decays. The EMC is used to select events on the basis of the centrality of the collision and fluctuations in E\({}_{\rm F}\). The EMC, together with the CTB, can be used to select events with unusual ratios of E\({}_{\rm F}\) to charged multiplicity.

One purpose of the TPC MWPC's is to trigger on the electron avalanche at the anode wires. The direct readout of this information allows triggering on fluctuations in changing multiplicity per change in pseudorapidity.

The VTC's are placed beyond the first dipole magnets in the beam (about seventeen meters). These provide information on the impact parameter independent of the E\({}_{\rm F}\) values. By looking at the well-separated proton and neutron spectators from the interaction point the spectator energy can be detected.

