## Appendix B Rapidity

The rapidity variable "y" is a useful variable used to describe the kinematic condition of a particle. The rapidity of a particle is defined in terms of its energy and momentum components, \(p_{0}\) and \(p_{z_{i}}\) by

\[y=\frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{p_{0}+p_{z}}{p_{0}-p_{z}}\right)\{\{\}\}.\]

It is a dimensionless quantity which is related to the ratio of forward momentum to the backward momentum. The rapidity of a particle in one frame of reference is related to the rapidity of the same particle in another frame of reference by an additive constant as the following derivation shows.

Consider a particle in a reference frame \(F\) with rapidity \(y\) and rapidity \(y^{*}\) in a Lorentz frame \(F^{\prime}\) which moves with a velocity \(\beta\) in the z-direction. The rapidity \(y^{*}\) of the particle in the \(F^{\prime}\) frame is

\[y^{*}=\frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{p_{0}+p_{z}}{p_{0}-p_{z}}\right)\{\left(2 \right)\}.\]

Under the Lorentz transformation, the energy \(p_{0}\) and the longitudinal momentum \(\vec{p_{z}}\) in the \(F^{\prime}\) reference frame are related to the energy \(p_{0}\) and longitudinal momentum \(p_{z}\) in the frame \(F\) by

\[\vec{p_{0}}=\gamma\{\vec{p_{0}}-\beta p_{z}\} \tag{3a}\] \[\vec{p_{z}}=\gamma\{\vec{p_{z}}-\beta p_{0}\} \tag{3b}\]

where \(\beta\) is the velocity of \(F\) relative to \(F\). Now substituting (3a) and (3b) into (2) we get

\[\vec{y}^{*}=\frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{\gamma\{p_{0},-\beta p_{z}\}+\gamma\{p _{z},-\beta p_{0}\}}{\gamma\{p_{0},-\beta p_{z}\}-\gamma\{p_{z},-\beta p_{0}\} }\right).\]\[y:=\frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{\gamma\left(1-\beta\right)}{\gamma\left(1+\beta \right)\left(P_{0}-P_{k}\right)}\right)\left(4\right).\]

Now substituting in (1), the rapidity of the particle in the frame \(F\), we get

\[y:=y+\frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{\gamma\left(1-\beta\right)}{\gamma\left(1+\beta \right)}\right)\left(5\right).\]

This is the additive relation of the rapidity between two reference frames.

**Pseudorapidity**.

To measure the rapidity of a particle, it is necessary to measure two quantities of the particle, such as outlined above. In many experiments, it is only possible to measure the angle of the particle's trajectory with the beam axis. In this case, it is convenient to utilize this information by employing the pseudorapidity variable \(\eta\). The pseudorapidity is defined as

\[\eta=\mbox{-}\ln\left[\tan\left(\frac{\beta}{2}\right)\right]\left(6\right),\]

where \(0\) is the angle between the particle momentum and the beam axis. In terms of the momentum the pseudorapidity can be written as

\[\eta=\frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{|p|+p_{k}}{|p|-P_{k}}\right)\left(7\right),\]

By comparing equations. (2) and (7), it is seen that the pseudorapidity coincides with the rapidity when the momentum is large, i.e. when \(|p|+p_{k}\). So the pseudorapidity is a convenient tool to define locations in the experiment. This is actually a limit of the rapidity for high energy particles. So the pseudorapidity is a convenient tool to define the angle of the particle's momentum with the beam axis.

